Chapter: Cce Builtin Bounds

 A call to is-letter reaches one of two implementations, and the chapter's
 cite list decides which: a chapter citing Foreword chapter CCE resolves
 the chapter definition, and a chapter citing nothing reaches the compiler
 builtin (emit-is-letter-builtin). The two are one band-pair that must not
 drift apart, and the compiler reaches the predicate only through the CCE
 definition, so the builtin needs its own runner. This chapter therefore
 deliberately cites nothing.

 The probe codes are the three that separate the bands, plus a digit: 12
 (a digit), 15 (the letter a), 70 (punctuation, the hole between the
 bands), 97 (accented e, the widened band). The 70 cell is the control
 that matters: a rebuilt builtin that swallowed the punctuation hole
 answers T there.

 is-whitespace is here for the same reason and is the same kind of claim:
 the band is 1..2 and NUL sits below it, so the 0 cell is what separates a
 corrected builtin from the `<= 2` one it replaced.

 Because this chapter cites nothing it is also the only probe that can see
 the ARM64 and RISC-V plugs disagree with x86. A chapter that cites CCE
 answers from the chapter definition on every target and hides the whole
 divergence; run this one under build/test-cross.ps1 after touching either
 plug's is-char-class dispatch.

Section: Cells

  cbb-letter : Integer -> Text
  cbb-letter (n) = if is-letter (code-to-char n) then "T" else "F"

  cbb-ws : Integer -> Text
  cbb-ws (n) = if is-whitespace (code-to-char n) then "T" else "F"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("builtin-letter 12,15,70,97: " & cbb-letter 12 & cbb-letter 15 & cbb-letter 70 & cbb-letter 97)
   print-line-uni ("builtin-ws 0,1,2,3: " & cbb-ws 0 & cbb-ws 1 & cbb-ws 2 & cbb-ws 3)
  end

